<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inle Lake</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inle Lake is a freshwater lake in the hills of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a>, Myanmar, at about 880 metres above sea level. It is home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intha">Intha</a> people, who fish with a distinctive leg-rowing style and grow crops on floating gardens. In the past it lay within the Shan state of Yawnghwe (Nyaungshwe), and it is famous for the Phaung Daw Oo pagoda and its annual festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inle-lake.jpg" alt="Inle Lake" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marc Veraart from Haarlem, Netherlands · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inle_Lake_(Myanmar).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Inle Lake lies in the Nyaungshwe Township of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a>, in the Shan Hills. It is the second largest lake in Myanmar, with an estimated surface area of 116 square kilometres, and one of the country's highest, at 880 metres. The average water depth in the dry season is 2.1 metres, and the deepest point is 3.7 metres. The lake drains through the Nam Pilu at its southern end. Large sections are covered by floating plants, and its clear waters hold more than 35 native fish species, 17 of them endemic. The climate is tropical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a>, with a rainy season from June to October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The people of the lake, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intha">Intha</a>, live in four towns bordering it, in small villages along its shores and on the water itself. Most are devout Buddhists and live in houses of wood and woven bamboo on stilts, and are largely self-sufficient farmers. Fishermen are known for a distinctive rowing style, standing at the stern on one leg with the other leg wrapped round the oar. It arose because the reeds and floating plants made it hard to see ahead from a seat, and only men practise it.</p><p>The lake dwellers also grow vegetables and fruit in gardens floating on the surface. Farmers gather weeds from the lake bottom and make them into beds anchored by bamboo poles. The gardens rise and fall with the water level, so are resistant to flooding. Between 1935 and 2000 the open water shrank by 32.4 per cent, and about 93 per cent of the loss is thought to be due to floating-garden agriculture, a practice introduced in the 1960s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The lake area has an established weaving industry. It produces hand-woven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> fabrics known as Inle longyi, and a lotus silk made only at Inle Lake and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, which was used for robes for the Buddha. The Hpaung Daw U Festival, which lasts 18 days, falls between August and October, and its boat races, with teams of leg-rowers in Shan dress, are among the most anticipated events. In 2015 the lake became Myanmar's first biosphere reserve, and since 2018 it has been a Ramsar site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inle Lake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Yawnghwe, Burmese Nyaungshwe, lay at the northern end of <b>Inle Lake</b> in the southern Shan plateau.</blockquote><a href="/state/yawnghwe-state">Yawnghwe State</a></li></ul></section>
